# Class and state diagram variants for block 1

> Exemplars of block 1's diagrams for shapes the base template (`domain-spec.md.example`, a
> root with value objects and a three-value lifecycle) does not show. Each variant carries
> the excerpt of the partial it is drawn from. The diagram names nothing that excerpt does
> not.

## Variant 1 · Child entity with multiplicity, reference by id

**Aggregate root:** `Order`. An order has one or more lines, owned by the order and never
reached from outside it. The customer enters by **reference (id)**: `Customer` is another
aggregate.

| Field | Type | Why this type |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | `OrderId` | Value object over `UUID` |
| `customerId` | `CustomerId` | Reference to the `Customer` aggregate |
| `lines` | `List<OrderLine>` | Child entity, one or more, built only through the root |
| `total` | `Money` | Amount and currency together |

| VO | Wraps | Own invariant |
|---|---|---|
| `OrderId` | `UUID` | not null |
| `CustomerId` | `UUID` | not null |
| `Money` | `BigDecimal`, `Currency` | amount ≥ 0, scale of the currency |

`OrderLine` is an entity with fields `sku` (`Sku`), `quantity` (`int`) and `price` (`Money`).

```mermaid
classDiagram
    direction LR
    class Order {
        OrderId id
        CustomerId customerId
        List~OrderLine~ lines
        Money total
    }
    class OrderLine {
        Sku sku
        int quantity
        Money price
    }
    Order *-- OrderId
    Order *-- CustomerId : Customer by reference
    Order "1" *-- "1..*" OrderLine : lines
    Order *-- Money
    OrderLine *-- Sku
    OrderLine *-- Money
```

The multiplicity is drawn because the text states it ("one or more"). `Customer` is a label,
never a class: the order holds its id, not the aggregate.

## Variant 2 · Sealed type instead of an optional field

**Aggregate root:** `User`. The department is either assigned or not, and the partial decides
a sealed `UserDepartment` with two permitted records, `Assigned` (holding `departmentId`, a
`DepartmentId`) and `Unassigned` — no `null`, no `Optional` on a field.

| Field | Type | Why this type |
|---|---|---|
| `id` | `UserId` | Value object over `UUID` |
| `department` | `UserDepartment` | Sealed: `Assigned` or `Unassigned` |

```mermaid
classDiagram
    direction LR
    class User {
        UserId id
        UserDepartment department
    }
    class UserDepartment {
        <<interface>>
    }
    class Assigned {
        DepartmentId departmentId
    }
    class Unassigned
    User *-- UserId
    User *-- UserDepartment
    Assigned ..|> UserDepartment
    Unassigned ..|> UserDepartment
```

## Variant 3 · Lifecycle with final states and a shared transition

| State field | Value | Reached by | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| `status` | `PLACED` | `Order.place(...)` — the creation factory | this case |
| `status` | `CONFIRMED` | `Order.confirm()` — from `PLACED` | this case |
| `status` | `SHIPPED` | `Order.ship(...)` — from `CONFIRMED` | `UC-121-ship-order` (backlog) |
| `status` | `CANCELLED` | `Order.cancel(reason)` — from `PLACED` or `CONFIRMED` | this case |

`SHIPPED` and `CANCELLED` are final: no method leaves them.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> PLACED : place
    PLACED --> CONFIRMED : confirm
    CONFIRMED --> SHIPPED : ship
    PLACED --> CANCELLED : cancel
    CONFIRMED --> CANCELLED : cancel
    SHIPPED --> [*]
    CANCELLED --> [*]
```

`cancel` is drawn twice because the text gives it two origins. The arrows to `[*]` are drawn
because the text says the two values are final — without that sentence, they stay out.

